WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.920
"Bones'" squints were known for being pretty&nbsp;
open-minded about new people and experiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.680
That is, until they weren’t. Keep&nbsp;
watching for the "Bones" scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.520
that makes us uncomfortable&nbsp;
for all the wrong reasons.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.400
One of the best things about "Bones"&nbsp;
is the even-handed, accepting nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.760
of the Jeffersonian staff when confronted with&nbsp;
lifestyles they've not previously considered.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.480
It makes sense that Temperance Brennan would&nbsp;
keep an open mind when thrust into a new culture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.360
Brennan is a forensic anthropologist, but you&nbsp;
don't get to that profession without studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.480
some cultural anthropology along the way. She&nbsp;
knew a lot more about other cultures than her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:45.040
colleagues, and she wasn't afraid of shoving&nbsp;
that fact into people's faces. She was a smart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.320
confident woman who didn't pipe down, making her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.080
one of the strongest female protagonists to&nbsp;
come out of television in the 21st century.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.240
FBI agent Booth, on the other hand, usually has a&nbsp;
more reactionary view of the things he's seeing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.840
But to his credit, he didn't feel put down when&nbsp;
Temperance knew more than him. He accepted it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:12.080
a fact, but these two viewpoints still frequently&nbsp;
came in conflict, with the show eventually coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:19.360
down in Bones' favor most of the time. But&nbsp;
one time the show just completely whiffed it.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:26.400
Season 4's episode titled "The Girl in the Mask"&nbsp;
is wild to behold after years of gender discourse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.280
In the episode, a Japanese forensics&nbsp;
expert, Dr. Haru Tanaka travels to DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.720
to help Booth investigate a death. Dr.&nbsp;
Tanaka dresses very androgynously and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.760
the squints obsess over what&nbsp;
their, quote, "real" gender is.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:42.720
"So, any guesses?"

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.320
"We don't really have enough evidence yet."

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.200
"No, not the case - the doctor. Dude or dudette?"

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.400
The scene that goes too far lands near the&nbsp;
end of the episode. Fed up with not knowing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.880
Angela hugs Dr. Tanaka until they have&nbsp;
a... biological reaction to being so close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.560
to a beautiful woman. Angela thus&nbsp;
concludes that Dr. Tanaka is a man.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:16.560
Not only is this extremely invasive, but the team&nbsp;
ignores the possibility that Dr. Tanaka could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:23.280
agender or nonbinary. Nobody just asks&nbsp;
Dr. Tanaka what their pronouns are, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.520
would be the polite thing to do. Brennan does&nbsp;
an excellent job of never gendering Dr. Tanaka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.960
always referring to them by their full name to&nbsp;
obviate the need for gendered pronouns at all.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.040
The misgendering of Dr. Tanaka&nbsp;
has its roots in orientalism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.080
The episode scores some points when Sweets says&nbsp;
Dr. Tanaka belongs to the "kei" subculture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.920
which is known for its genderless style, and&nbsp;
that Tanaka's gender shouldn't matter. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.080
the rest of the team essentially dismisses&nbsp;
Dr. Tanaka's gender expression as a Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:03.120
thing. But plenty of people all over the world&nbsp;
identify as nonbinary, and what could have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:10.720
an excellent opportunity for representation on TV&nbsp;
just ended up being problematic and disrespectful.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.720
"The Girl in the Mask" only came out in 2009,&nbsp;
which isn't that long ago. Yet, it manages to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.760
ultimately damage androgynous representation&nbsp;
on-screen by insisting upon the significance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.720
of assigning everyone a binary gender role.&nbsp;
In an interview after the episode came out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.800
Ally Maki, who portrays Tanaka, spoke&nbsp;
about how that division emphasizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.200
a clear cultural divide between&nbsp;
Western and Eastern sensibilities.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.640
Maki discusses how she was at&nbsp;
first hesitant to audition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.160
as well as how the creative team lowered&nbsp;
her voice register in post-production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.440
She also provides a different type of insight&nbsp;
into the character and how, strangely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.800
the episode actually confronts Western&nbsp;
culture's ideas on gender. Maki explains -

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.720
"I thought it was hilarious that the&nbsp;
three [characters] wanted to know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.040
'Man or woman, man or woman.'&nbsp;
It was so important to them."

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.680
Maki, who is Japanese-American, goes on to&nbsp;
clarify how Japan is far more accepting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:13.200
androgynous identities that don't neatly&nbsp;
fit into binaries. She goes on to state -

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.920
"In Japan, you know, it's like no one would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.800
really care. You're walking down the&nbsp;
street, and it wouldn't even be an issue."

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.880
Whether the episode succeeded in getting that idea&nbsp;
across is open to interpretation, but it's clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.880
Japan has been ahead of the curve when it comes&nbsp;
to accepting individuals of all gender identities.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:39.360
Few would argue the episode could've done more&nbsp;
to be more inclusive of non-binary identities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:45.440
perhaps by casting a non-binary actor in the role.&nbsp;
While television still has a long way to go with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:53.760
greater LGBTQ+ representation, we're in a much&nbsp;
better place today than we were even 10 years ago.

